  "final_text": "All four steps are done, and the rule read back as enabled.\n\n1. **Data view:** 'Kibana Sample Data Logs' (id `90943e30-9a47-11e8-b64d-95841ca0b247`) covers the index `kibana_sample_data_logs`. Its time field is `timestamp`. It has about 45 fields, including `response` (text), `response.keyword`, `bytes`, `clientip`, `geo.*`, `machine.os` and `url`.\n2. **Dashboard:** 'S4-5ac75f5c Web logs' (id `b0edfccf-d489-4c8d-ab08-54d333d27180`) has two panels. One is a line chart, \"Requests over time\" (count by `timestamp`). The other is a bar chart of request count by `response.keyword`.\n3. **Alert rule:** 'S4-5ac75f5c 5xx errors' is an Elasticsearch query rule on `kibana_sample_data_logs`.\n   - It checks every minute and fires when the count is above 10 in a 5-minute window.\n   - It counts requests whose `response.keyword` is between \"500\" and \"599\". That is a string range, which works because response codes are always three digits.\n   - It was created enabled, so I didn't need to enable it afterwards.\n   - The rule has no actions, so when it fires it won't send a notification.\n4. **Panel edit:** the response-code panel is now titled 'Responses by code'. The read-back showed `enabled: true`, and the last execution status was `ok`.\n\nTIME_FIELD: timestamp\nRULE_ENABLED: yes",
